\name{BasketStore}
\alias{BasketStore}
\title{The collection of baskets kept in the project's MongoDB}
\description{
UBI knows nothing about what an index or a fund holds, so baskets are kept in this project's own MongoDB, in the \code{asset_baskets} collection of the database \code{Configuration} names. Each document is one version of one basket, identified by its \code{name} and the \code{effective_date} it takes effect, because an index is rebalanced and a fund's holdings change every month; \code{load()} finds the version in effect on a given day. A document names each member by its UBI \code{instrument_id} beside its readable identity fields, and \code{load()} rebuilds every member in one list request.

The \code{kind} field of a document decides which class \code{load()} builds: \code{portfolio}, \code{watchlist}, \code{index}, \code{exchange_traded_fund_constituents}, \code{mutual_fund_constituents}, or \code{basket} for a plain \code{AssetBasket}. \code{load_for_instrument()} finds the basket whose \code{linked_instrument_id} is a given instrument, which is how \code{constituents} on an index or a fund finds its contents.

The store shares its collection and document shape with the Python library \code{tradingmachine}, so a basket saved from Python loads here and the other way round. Dates are stored as \code{"YYYY-MM-DD"} text and \code{updated_at} as a MongoDB date, exactly as Python stores them.
}
